# Elixir Koans - 20 Comprehensions

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
```

## Intro

A comprehension is made of three parts: generators, filters, and collectibles. We will look at how these interact with each other

## The generator, `n <- [1, 2, 3, 4]`, is providing the values for our comprehension

```elixir
assert for(n <- [1, 2, 3, 4], do: n * n) == ___
```

## Any enumerable can be a generator

```elixir
assert for(n <- 1..4, do: n * n) == ___
```

## A generator specifies how to extract values from a collection

```elixir
collection = [["Hello", "World"], ["Apple", "Pie"]]
```

```elixir
assert for([a, b] <- collection, do: "#{a} #{b}") == ___
```

## You can use multiple generators at once

```elixir
assert for(x <- ["little", "big"], y <- ["dogs", "cats"], do: "#{x} #{y}") == ___
```

## Use a filter to reduce your work

```elixir
assert for(n <- [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], n > 3, do: n) == ___
```

## Add the result of a comprehension to an existing collection

```elixir
collection = for x <- ["Pecan", "Pumpkin"], into: %{}, do: {x, "#{x} Pie"}
```

```elixir
assert collection == ___
```

